            <div xml:id="d610">

                <pb facs="1086" n="1038" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v24/pg1038"/>

                <head>610. Memorandum From Robert W. Komer of the National Security Council Staff to
                    the Executive Secretary of the Department of State (Read)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d610-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1965-09-02">September 2, 1965</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>US Policy Toward South Africa—Status Report on NSAM 295</item>
                </list>
                <p>The July 31, 1965 Status Report on NSAM 295<anchor n="2" xml:id="d610-f2"/>
                    raises some questions which deserve a close look.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Would US policy objectives be furthered if we were to enlist the support
                        of friendly countries with diplomatic representation in South Africa to join
                        in US–UK approaches to the South African Government on apartheid and the
                        South West Africa case?</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Is there any political advantage to be gained for the US and its policy
                        objectives by transferring our space tracking facilities in South Africa
                        before events force us to do so?</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#bold">R. W. Komer</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
